WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.796 --> 00:00:07.040
PATRICK: Outer space is a great realm of&nbsp;
possibility and the question is whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.400
that realm of possibility will be closed off.
THOMPSON: We track twenty-five thousand plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.880
objects continuously to understand where they&nbsp;
are and if they pose a hazard to each other.
&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.520
PATRICK: There’s a real danger that there’s&nbsp;
going to be militarization of outer space.
&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680
BOLDEN: They’ve got to be able to go in and say,&nbsp;
look, I’ve got everything in my arsenal to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.440
wipe you out. But I don’t want to do&nbsp;
that. I don’t want to make this domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.880
unusable for everybody.
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:41.280
Space is an infinite horizon - an endless void&nbsp;
full of possibility. But the part of space near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.440
Earth is getting really crowded. And it’s getting&nbsp;
really dangerous. It used to be just Russia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.440
the United States, but now nine countries,&nbsp;
and dozens of private entities, are regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.280
launching objects into space. All of these players&nbsp;
are competing for limited real estate. Crowding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.120
leads to conflict. And so far the world hasn’t&nbsp;
developed rules to solve space disputes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.280
or to prevent war from spilling over into the&nbsp;
night sky. As things heat up, some countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.400
are pouring billions of dollars into space&nbsp;
defense. The technologies are futuristic-sounding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:23.360
anti-satellite lasers high-altitude EMP&nbsp;
weapons. But the biggest danger is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:30.560
slightly less glamorous ... space junk. I’m&nbsp;
Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.920
Today, will the world ruin space?  
PATRICK:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.200
Space junk, some of it’s like the pieces of&nbsp;
defunct satellites, including those that have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.040
destroyed by anti-satellite weapons tests. 
SIERRA: This is Stewart Patrick. He’s a senior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.280
fellow for Global Governance here at&nbsp;
the Council. That means he spends most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.720
of his time trying to figure out how&nbsp;
to get the world to work together. 
&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.000
PATRICK: But basically, there are these pieces of&nbsp;
space junk and they’re hurtling around at almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.160
twenty thousand miles an hour in low-Earth orbit.&nbsp;
And I don’t know if you saw the movie Gravity, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.680
what happens is that if those things collide with&nbsp;
a functioning satellite or, say, a space station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.160
the results could be absolutely catastrophic.
 
SIERRA: Just rocket Sandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.560
Bullock out into space.
PATRICK: Exactly. Totally. Yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.000
And there are also seven hundred and fifty&nbsp;
thousand pieces that are being tracked that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.080
larger than a pea but smaller than a softball. So&nbsp;
any one of those could cause catastrophic damage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.720
And so the question is, how do we deal with all&nbsp;
this space junk? Because if there’s if there’s too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.600
much of an accumulation of space junk, it could&nbsp;
render this outer space domain almost unusable.
&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.160
SIERRA: Time for a quick zero gravity simulation.&nbsp;
So imagine that every time you threw away an apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.400
core, or tossed your bag on the couch, it didn’t&nbsp;
land. Instead it just stayed in motion alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.320
you. Eventually you’d be surrounded by stuff.&nbsp;
And that would make getting around pretty hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.760
That’s basically what happens in space. Everything&nbsp;
stays at the orbit it’s in, moving at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.720
speed, until something else touches it. And&nbsp;
there’s so much of it out there that getting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.640
and around space is dangerous. 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.640
So I’ve heard that Space Command provides a&nbsp;
type of space junk air traffic control. 
&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.840
THOMPSON: That’s correct. 
SIERRA: This is Lt General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.200
David Thompson. He’s the Vice Commander of&nbsp;
Air Force Space Command. He helps oversee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.040
the use of space by the U.S. military.
THOMPSON: We operate a global set of sensors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.960
We track twenty-five thousand plus objects&nbsp;
continuously to understand where they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.400
and if they pose a hazard to each other. And&nbsp;
if they pose a hazard, we specifically provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:48.640
warning to anybody. It might be NASA, it might be&nbsp;
a commercial operator, it might be the Russians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.600
or the Chinese. We provide warning if that’s&nbsp;
a hazard. We do it because today we’re really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.200
the only folks in the world that can do it. 
SIERRA: I mean, because an object the size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.480
your fist moving that quickly could be incredibly&nbsp;
destructive to anything, including a satellite.
&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.120
THOMPSON: Correct. Absolutely. In fact, objects&nbsp;
a whole lot smaller than that at that speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.000
create incredible danger. There’s a story—actually&nbsp;
a true story of the space shuttle back when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.240
was flying in its early days in the 1990s. It had&nbsp;
multi-paned windows, a fleck of paint traveling at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.800
seventeen thousand miles an hour went through&nbsp;
all but the last pane of that window.
&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:23.885
SIERRA: A fleck of paint?
THOMPSON: A fleck of paint.
&nbsp;

00:04:23.885 --> 00:04:24.640
SIERRA: Wow.
THOMPSON: Had it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.480
penetrated that last window&nbsp;
pane, then the atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.520
right—the air inside the shuttle would have been&nbsp;
evacuated and they would have had a huge problem.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:38.800
SIERRA: So, where does space junk come from?&nbsp;
Some of it is created when satellites go dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.080
or malfunction, or break up. This is sort&nbsp;
of inevitable and out of our control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.160
But the other way space junk is created is&nbsp;
more intentional. Since the 1960s,the US and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.160
Russia have been testing their ability to destroy&nbsp;
satellites in orbit. And recently, other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.760
have been doing the same. In 2007, for example,&nbsp;
China shocked the world by destroying one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.560
their own weather satellites with a missile. 
RUPPERSBERGER: I’m very concerned that China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.440
has conducted a destructive anti satellite&nbsp;
test against its own satellite, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.680
redfield as a result of this was troubling&nbsp;
to our satellites and our space missions.
&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:22.400
Patrick MURPHY:India held an anti-satellite&nbsp;
test, now this test created a cloud of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.280
orbital debris, thats flying in low earth orbit 
Jim BRIDENSTINE: That is a terrible terrible thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.560
to create an event that sends debris in an&nbsp;
apogee that goes above the intl space station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.600
that kind of activity is not compatible&nbsp;
with the future of human space flight that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.720
we need to see have happen.
SIERRA: Why would it matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:49.440
if someone shot down their own satellite? 
THOMPSON: The problem is debris. That 2007 test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:58.240
that the Chinese executed created more than 3,500&nbsp;
pieces of debris. Ninety percent of that debris is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.160
still in orbit today and poses a threat to all&nbsp;
other satellites operating in that regime.
&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.480
SIERRA: So if people keep&nbsp;
doing these sorts of tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.720
that number will just keep going up and up? 
THOMPSON: Correct. Correct. That number will grow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.360
Those satellites in lower orbits—eventually&nbsp;
that debris will reenter the atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.560
but most of the operating location of the&nbsp;
satellites that would be under threat—you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.960
we are talking about decades and perhaps centuries&nbsp;
that the debris would remain in orbit. 
&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.040
SIERRA: When you look up at the night sky and&nbsp;
see a satellite blinking by, it’s probably in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:39.920
something called low earth orbit. This refers to&nbsp;
the first 1,200 miles of space above the earth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.440
surface. Some specialized satellites orbit&nbsp;
higher than that, but the majority stay pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.840
close to Earth. Theorists worry that conflict&nbsp;
in space, or a cascade of satellite collisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.520
could turn low earth orbit into a deadly&nbsp;
traffic jam of debris. If that happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.920
there’d be no way to get through the junk and&nbsp;
into outer space. That means no new satellites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.360
no trips to the moon, no Mars missions. And&nbsp;
the problem could last for hundreds of years. 

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.200
SIERRA: Is the idea to just sort of&nbsp;
flex a muscle and show that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.400
shoot down a satellite if you want to?
THOMPSON: They used it as a target just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:24.000
demonstrate the capability. Because space is&nbsp;
such an important part of military operations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.640
it provides tremendous advantages to the armed&nbsp;
forces of the United States and would to anybody.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.440
And so naturally, someone who anticipates they&nbsp;
may end up in a conflict with the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.280
they may think the first thing they need to do in&nbsp;
a war is remove those space capabilities so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.640
it impacts the U.S.’ ability to&nbsp;
operate. So there is military advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.480
to denying use of space to&nbsp;
an adversary in conflict.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.240
SIERRA: So what would the U.S. military look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.880
like without satellites.
THOMPSON: The U.S. military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.240
would look like the military we had in the&nbsp;
twentieth century, industrial age warfare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.280
As a couple of examples, at the end of World&nbsp;
War II we had a tremendous strategic Air Force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.120
That Air Force put a thousand airplanes in the&nbsp;
air over Europe, over Germany every single day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.000
at the end of the war, dropped anywhere from&nbsp;
six (thousand) to twelve thousand bombs, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.920
aimed at one point. But at the end of the—at the&nbsp;
end of the war the U.S. Strategic Bombing Survey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:22.480
said, of those nine (thousand) to twelve thousand&nbsp;
bombs, only one hundred fell within a half mile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:30.000
of the target. That’s industrial age warfare. In&nbsp;
Vietnam, there was a strategic railroad crossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.320
of a river in North Vietnam, took thirty airplanes&nbsp;
and 176 bombs to destroy one railroad bridge. 
&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.960
SIERRA: And Now? 
THOMPSON: Now we’re talking about two bombers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:43.680
108 bombs, 108 different targets.
SIERRA: Without satellites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.080
U.S. military operations might look like&nbsp;
something you’d see on the History Channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:53.280
And that’s because satellites are part of nearly&nbsp;
everything the modern military does. It’s not hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.400
to see why an ambitious enemy would want to gain&nbsp;
the ability to shoot them down, despite the risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:06.000
that space junk poses to everyone on the planet.
THOMPSON: Satellite communications allow us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.760
communicate anywhere on the globe at any time.&nbsp;
Whether you are in the middle of an ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:15.200
in the middle of a desert, in air space anywhere&nbsp;
in the world. We certainly use satellites to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:21.280
collect intelligence information from space, and&nbsp;
do that all over the world all the time. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:28.720
also, things like missile warning. It’s&nbsp;
also a matter of not creating undue damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.920
on civilian populations, not striking targets&nbsp;
you are not aiming at which would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.880
undue effect on civilian populations, civilian&nbsp;
infrastructure, and things like that.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:44.560
SIERRA:This all may seem pretty removed from our&nbsp;
daily lives. But a lot of what satellites do for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.840
the military, they do for the rest of us too. And&nbsp;
our world has become deeply dependent on them. 
&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:56.240
BOLDEN: One of the things we love to do, and&nbsp;
we’ve never been able to do it worldwide, is get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:03.120
people to join us in a day without space. 
