organization that actually would be able to&nbsp;
go in and verify whether or not commitments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:48.800
are actually being made. And that that will&nbsp;
require a level of intrusiveness that is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:52.560
going to bother potential adversaries of&nbsp;
ours, because we get to look at their stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:58.240
and they get to look at our stuff, but a lot of&nbsp;
sovereignty-minded leaders and politicians and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.520
officials in the United States will resist that&nbsp;
because they may not be willing to trade off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:08.240
that level of transparency, which they think might&nbsp;
give up, you know, some of the state secrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:14.160
in the interest of creating a stable outer-space&nbsp;
realm. But it’s only going to get more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:20.560
competitive and contested if we don’t have some&nbsp;
rules of the road for outer space. It will become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:25.120
the Wild West, in effect, in outer space.
SIERRA: And the benefits are no joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.200
serious science gets done in space. For example,&nbsp;
most of our understanding of climate change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.240
comes from satellites. At any given time,&nbsp;
researchers are conducting scientific and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:41.760
medical experiments in space that couldn’t&nbsp;
be done anywhere else. Plus, cooperation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:46.880
in space has helped the U.S. form important&nbsp;
ties. Even with countries that have a tense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:54.880
relationship with the U.S. down on Earth. 
SIERRA: How closely do NASA scientists work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:58.000
scientists from other countries?
BOLDEN: NASA scientists and scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:04.000
from other countries try to the greatest&nbsp;
extent to work incredibly closely. When I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:10.640
the NASA administrator under President Obama, he&nbsp;
literally charged NASA to, the way he phrased it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:18.800
expand the number of nontraditional partners that&nbsp;
you have across the NASA portfolio. And so, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:24.400
picked a great example when you mentioned the U.S.&nbsp;
and China. In my role as the NASA administrator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.960
my job was to do everything in my power to&nbsp;
find a way to enable our countries to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:39.120
collaboratively on peaceful things, hoping&nbsp;
that if we could do that then it would delay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:45.120
if not prevent conflict. I tell people all&nbsp;
the time, I think next to the Department of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:53.040
State and the Department of Defense NASA is the&nbsp;
greatest provider of soft power for the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:59.120
you know, in our arsenal. So we really work as&nbsp;
hard as we can to try to facilitate peaceful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:05.040
collaboration among nations. If you look at&nbsp;
the relationship between NASA and Roscosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:11.200
the Russian space agency, it is a perfect&nbsp;
example of the way that NASA uses its soft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:19.200
power to try to eliminate the tensions or ease the&nbsp;
tension that comes about almost daily with Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:25.360
in terms of diplomatic relationship. China,&nbsp;
same thing, we work with China in lunar science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.400
study of the moon. And we work with them in&nbsp;
geodetics, which is the study of earthquakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:34.720
They’re really, really good at it. And so&nbsp;
we try to learn from them, and we try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:39.360
use their data and they use our data. 
SIERRA: But as things heat up in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:44.800
it’s feeling less and less like a place for&nbsp;
peaceful cooperation. And if things worsen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:49.440
these scientific bonds could break.
Patrick: One of the big trends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:55.280
and a worrisome trend, is the fact that other&nbsp;
countries are following the U.S. lead in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:00.560
creating space forces as part of their military&nbsp;
establishment. Recently the French government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:05.120
has announced that it is also going to&nbsp;
create a space force. There are similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:12.240
military doctrines and military instruments&nbsp;
being developed in China, in Russia. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:18.080
not be too surprised to see the Indians follow&nbsp;
suit. It sort of reminds me of the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:25.120
nuclear age, where there’s a pell-mell desire&nbsp;
to sort of create one’s own capabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:29.440
How can we take advantage of this to make&nbsp;
sure that we are not threatened by others? But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:34.720
by building up those capabilities, then of course&nbsp;
you, by definition, make others insecure. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:38.320
you often get these arms races as a result.
SIERRA: Do you think if we dialed it back they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.720
would too?
PATRICK:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:43.600
You know, a lot of skeptics&nbsp;
of my arguments would say no.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:48.800
I think that number of folks self-styled&nbsp;
realists would say, if it’s not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:52.720
to be us, it’s going to be somebody else. 
BOLDEN: It goes back to the trust but verify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.600 --> 00:19:00.320
I’m not sure that we have a way yet to verify that&nbsp;
hostile nations, quote/unquote “hostile” nations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:05.040
or nations that we’re not exactly sure we&nbsp;
trust in everything, I’m not sure that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:09.840
have a way yet to verify that they’re going to&nbsp;
comply with the convention. And so we say, OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:15.120
we just don’t want to do it at all. But people in&nbsp;
the national security apparatus have got to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:22.880
much better negotiators and, you know,&nbsp;
consensus-builders than they’ve ever been before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:27.520
They’ve got to be able to go in and say, look,&nbsp;
I’ve got everything in my arsenal to just wipe you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:33.280
out. But I don’t want to do that. And I don’t want&nbsp;
to make this domain unusable for everybody. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:39.280
don’t make me do that. Let’s reach some agreement&nbsp;
as to what we’ll use and what the ground rules are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:47.440
for its use, and what can we take out of the game?&nbsp;
I see more Star Trek than Star Wars. That’s what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.120
think people always ask, what’s your favorite,&nbsp;
Star Trek or Star Wars, because we have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:58.800
conditioned to view Star Trek as scientists and&nbsp;
explorers seeking after good, and Star Wars being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:02.640
people who are in constant war fighting, yeah.
SIERRA: So we want it to stay Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.560
Trek and stay away from Star Wars.
BOLDEN: We want it to stay Star Trek and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:08.800
stay away from Star Wars. Every once in a while,&nbsp;
you know, it’s necessary to put the shields up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.240
but we shouldn’t put the shields&nbsp;
up just because there’s somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:19.040
else out there who doesn’t look like us. 
SIERRA: Space is so essential to our daily lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:24.560
and to our security, that it seems like we&nbsp;
have no choice but to do whatever is necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:29.440
to protect it. But the rush to protect&nbsp;
something can itself lead to conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:37.040
Humankind has not, as of yet, exported war to&nbsp;
the cosmos. The thing about space is that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:43.040
won’t get many chances to get it right - whatever&nbsp;
damage we do could circle the earth for centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:48.240
The shields may already be up, but&nbsp;
there is still time to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:54.720
what sort of place we want space to be. 
SIERRA: What did you feel the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.720 --> 00:21:00.240
you went to space and looked back on Earth?
BOLDEN: I don’t have a good word because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:05.280
an overused word, and that’s “awed.” Just&nbsp;
looking at this planet from that vantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:11.360
point is awe-inspiring. You saw was the&nbsp;
thin blue line that is our atmosphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:18.240
You saw the ocean. And I always say it’s&nbsp;
one ocean. No borders, no boundaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:26.400
You get the understanding about humanity and&nbsp;
you become sort of an environmentalist. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:33.040
a pacifist, that’s not the right word;&nbsp;
you become someone who is an idealist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:41.520
You dream of the world the way it&nbsp;
should be as opposed to, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:44.840
the way it is. 
